l passed as quickly  as possible  michael couture global news ottawa  two years after convicted pedophile  jeffrey epstein died in jail while  awaiting trial for sex trafficking his  longtime companion is standing trial a  jury will determine whether gelain  maxwell was epstein's accomplice in  opening statements prosecutors described  maxwell as a predator saying she and  epstein were partners in crime the  59-year-old is charged with six counts  related to sex trafficking she is  accused of recruiting and grooming  underage girls for epstein to sexually  abuse the trial is expected to last over  a month and if convicted maxwell faces  up to 80 years in prison  twitter ceo jack dorsey has announced he  is leaving the social media platform he  helped create the 45 year co-founded  twitter in 2006 he announced his  departure in a tweet saying there aren't  many companies that get to this level  and there aren't many founders that  choose their company over their own ego  twitter shares soared with the news this  morning dorsey is also ceo of the  payment firm square and some investors  questioned whether he could be effective  leading both companies dorsey will be  replaced by twitter's chief technical  officer parag agrawal  next the spongy solution to make cities  more resilient to floods  [Music]  after every extreme weather event it  becomes clear we need to find ways to be  more resilient most of our cities and  our infrastructure are not designed to  handle record amounts of rainfall that  because of climate change is becoming  more common there's a new idea taking  root that harnesses the power of nature  and absorbs water like a sponge mike  drollet explains how it works  there's no one doing the damage in  british columbia weeks into an  unrelenting flood the hope is that it  doesn't get any worse  eventually the lost roads and houses  will be rebuilt but to avoid a repeat  urban planners say we need a dramatic  change  in the way we think  our concrete-laden cities have long been  developed on the premise that we can  control flooding with storm drains but  when it rains too much those drains get  overwhelmed to solve that problem  engineers have turned to nature  green space they've discovered acts like  a sponge soaking up in releasing water  slowly cities are  areas that a lot of the natural surfaces  have been covered with concrete or other  paved surfaces and what that in effect  does is it doesn't allow  water  including rain water to go where it  naturally would which is  below the ground china didn't develop  the idea of the sponge city but they've  embraced it the city of chongqing for  one recently converted 99 hectares into  a wetland park which now acts like a  giant sponge but that's china where  change happens fast  well not as dramatic a transformation  toronto is redeveloping its portlands  with an eye towards increased future  flooding this desolate looking plot of  land will have an over abundance of  green space for aesthetics yes but  mostly necessity  toronto used to have a large system of  streams which naturally controlled  flooding but they've all been vanished  there's only so much city planners can  do when the neighborhoods we live in are  dominated by hard surfaces kitchener  ontario saw that problem and got  creative incentivizing residents to  lower their storm water tax by creating  more green space on their properties  there can be debate about the cause of  climate change but i don't think there's  a lot of debate about the fact that it  is occurring so  in order to try to mitigate  any negative impacts from  from those changes  we are implementing strategies today in  order to protect ourselves in the future  you could say they're trying to stay dry  by soaking up new ideas microlight  global news toronto  next why barbados is severing ties with  the queen  [Music]  sweden's political roller coaster took  another big turn today the country  reelected its prime minister who  resigned last week just hours after  taking the post magdalena anderson and  her social democrats won by a narrow  margin in a new vote today she will  attempt to lead until the election next  september anderson who was previously  finance minister had stepped down after  her two-party coalition collapsed over  her proposed budget  the caribbean nation of barbados is  starting a new chapter in its history  it's out with the queen and in with a  president the first commonwealth country  in nearly three decades to declare  itself a republic the split is amicable  though prince charles flew to barbados  to watch it all happen redmond shannon  explains why barbados is cutting ties  with the british monarchy  for barbados republicans this moment of  celebration is generations in the making  that colonial cloak  a wicked british joke  no longer cover us  sunset on the empire the first british  ship landed in barbados in 1625  hundreds of thousands of africans were  sent into slavery on the island's  plantations until emancipation in the  1800s it wasn't until the 1960s that  barbados became independent prime  minister mia motley taking the next step  in 2021 welcoming prince charles to the  event the man who would have been the  country's next head of state her job is  to bring barbados forward to the 21st  century  and to get our grip away from the slave  master grip but there is some unease  about how the formal process was pushed  through parliament without ever  consulting the public via referendum i  guess that the public they really had  much say on that for the government to  do this here to the people of barbados  it's very wrong and i see it as illegal  there are 15 other commonwealth realms  including canada 22 years ago australian  voters rejected a republic partly  because the proposal involved a  president elected by lawmakers rather  than voters barbados is adopting that  system with outgoing governor general  sandra mason becoming president so it's  possible that once barbados becomes a  republic we may see renewed calls for  that in jamaica as well royal historian  carolyn harris says any path to a  canadian republic is far more complex  even if polls suggest most canadians  think the monarchy is out of date and  it's very difficult for all ten  provinces to be on the same page at the  same time  also treaties with indigenous peoples or  with the crown as the crown fades from  prominence in barbados  there are at least some legacies of  empire that remain redmond shannon  global news london  and that is global national for this  monday i'm donna friesen tonight's your  canada is front knack provincial park  north of kingston ontario we'd love to  see your canada please email it to  viewers at globalnational.com  thanks for watching hope to see you here  again tomorrow take care of yourselves  bye  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you
 

